Title: "Command Headers"
Id: "8a8a6f91-8733-470f-b205-c7022aecfb7c"

Text: "Command headers define the shape of a command.

```mlg-fragment
\\some.command
\\foo{f(x_)}
\\foo{X ::= {x_ : ...}}
\\foo{G ::= (X, *, e)}
\\foo{G ::= (X ::= (a, b), f(x_))}
\\foo{a, b}(x_)
\\function:?on{A}:?to{B}
```

The `:?` marker makes a tail part optional. It is accepted only in command
**headers**, never in command expressions: `[\\function:on{A}:?to{B}]` declares
both `\\function:on` and `\\function:on:to`, and a use site writes whichever
optional parts it includes with a plain `:to`. Multiple optional tails may be
independently omitted as long as the remaining tails keep the order from the
heading, so `[\\foo:?baz{A}:?bar{B}]` accepts `\\foo`, `\\foo:baz`, `\\foo:bar`, and
`\\foo:baz:bar`, but not `\\foo:bar:baz`. Every tail part, optional or not, still
requires at least one `{...}` argument group.

Curly argument groups are required where the definition expects them. Trailing
parenthesized groups are invocation groups: if a definition includes only
trailing parenthesized groups beyond the actual use, the use may omit them, so
`[\\some.function{A}(x, y)]` may be referenced as either `\\some.function{A}` or
`\\some.function{A}(x, y)`.

Callable headings may use placeholder spelling for their trailing parameters,
matching function declarations, so `[\\natural.succ(n_)]` declares the same
one-argument shape as a trailing `(n)` group while making the parameter role
explicit. Infix command headings accept the same spelling for their operands.

Argument-group counts are tracked as the command's required shape but do not
disambiguate definitions: two definitions with the same signature and different
argument counts are still duplicate command signatures. The one exception is a
mapping-parameter header — see Mapping Parameter Headers.

A command expression may end with context suffixes after all ordinary curly and parenthesized arguments:

```mlg-fragment
\\ordered.pair#using{A := X; B := Y}
\\some.theorem#given{X is \\set; y \"in\" X}
```

`#using{...}` supplies values for a command's `using:` section. `#given{...}` supplies values for theorem-like `given:` sections. Arguments are separated by semicolons so each argument may contain commas."
Id: "eb46ad36-1acc-4efc-8e38-9ba2640dc4c3"
